  "worked_solution": "First, since $s={e}^{t}-1,$ we have that $t=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(s+1).$\n\nThe vector function that pametrizes the curve $r(t)$ by its arc length $s,$ with $s\\ge 0,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(s+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\u27e8{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(s+1)}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(s+1)],\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(s+1)}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(s+1)]\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(s+1)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(s+1)],\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(s+1)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(s+1)]\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the $x$-component of $R$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(s+1)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(s+1)].\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
